on":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f1.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing numbers of and trends in chemical incidents, by type, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. A total of 57,975 incidents occurred during 1999-2008; 41,993 (72%) occurred in a fixed facility, and 15,981 (28%) were transportation related. Incident type was missing for one incident. The total number of incidents varied, and the trend decreased overall (R2 = 0.3). This decrease was driven by fixed-facility incidents (R2 = 0.6) because of a slight upward trend that occurred in transportation incidents (R2 = 0.3).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":511,"renderedHeight":345,"naturalWidth":511,"naturalHeight":345,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 724 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":13084},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f2.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing number of and trends in persons injured in chemical incidents, by incident type, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. A total of 15,506 persons were injured; 13,502 were injured in fixed facilities, and 2,004 were injured in transportation-related incidents. The average number of injured persons per year was 1,551. The majority of injured persons were employees (7,674), followed by members of the general public (4,737), students (1,730), and responders (1,340); the population group was unknown for 25 injured persons.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":510,"renderedHeight":350,"naturalWidth":510,"naturalHeight":350,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 735 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":13427},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f3.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing number of and trends in persons injured in chemical incidents, by incident type, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. The trend for number of injured employees decreased slightly but was always higher than the other categories of general public, students, and responders (R2 = 0.3). The number of injured persons from the general public generally increased over time (R2 = 0.3). The number of injured responders decreased slightly (R2 = 0.5). The number of injured students was more variable.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":508,"renderedHeight":375,"naturalWidth":508,"naturalHeight":375,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 705 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":15868},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f4.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing number of and trends in deaths from chemical incidents, by incident type, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. A total of 354 deaths occurred during 1999-2008; 180 deaths were in fixed facilities and 174 were transportation related. The average number of deaths per year was 35. A spike in transportation-related deaths occurred in 2006. The trend for deaths increased (R2 = 0.7), which was driven by incidents in fixed facilities (R2 = 0.5).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":513,"renderedHeight":348,"naturalWidth":513,"naturalHeight":348,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 655 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":14539},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f5.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing number of and trends in deaths from chemical incidents, by category of injured person, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. An overall increase in deaths in the general public occurred (R2 = 0.9); no trends were found among employees or responders.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":503,"renderedHeight":347,"naturalWidth":503,"naturalHeight":347,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 461 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":14790},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f6.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing number of and trends in persons injured by chemical incidents and number of and trends in chemical incidents, by month, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. The number of incidents increased in the spring during March-June, and a decrease occurred in the remainder of the year (R2 = 0.5). The highest number of persons were injured in June (n = 1,683), and the fewest in December (n = 1,034); no trend was found.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":850,"renderedHeight":402,"naturalWidth":850,"naturalHeight":402,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 625 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":14179},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f7.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing number of and trends in persons injured by chemical incidents and number of and trends in chemical incidents, by day of the week, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. A decreasing trend in incidents occurred during Monday- Sunday (R2 = 0.7); this trend was similar for the number of injured persons (R2 = 0.6). ","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":840,"renderedHeight":393,"naturalWidth":840,"naturalHeight":393,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 523 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":11849},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/s6402a2f8.gif","alt":"The figure is a line graph showing number of and trends in persons injured by chemical incidents and number of and trends in chemical incidents, by time of day, reported in the nine states (Iowa, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Texas, Washington, and Wisconsin) that participated in the Hazardous Substances Emergency Events Surveillance system during 1999-2008. The highest number of incidents occurred earlier in the day (6:00 a.m.-11:59 a.m.) and then decreased as the day went on (R2 = 0.9). Approximately half as many incidents occurred during other times of the day, which was similar for the number of injured persons (R2 = 1.0).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":836,"renderedHeight":396,"naturalWidth":836,"naturalHeight":396,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 649 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":11545},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_word.gif","alt":"Microsoft Word file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":1087},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_excel.gif","alt":"Microsoft Excel file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":644},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_ppt.gif","alt":"Microsoft PowerPoint file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":676},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_wmv.gif","alt":"Audio/Video file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":nul